The validation data confirms the claims in the option cards. Let me now check the gap registry to verify the 674-absence count and the structure, and glance at a zero-scorable crop to confirm the product viability question.
Good — both zero-scorable crops are confirmed to contain those absence markers. I now have enough grounding to write the critique. Let me verify one more thing: Option C's structural claim about document heterogeneity, by quickly checking the size disparity (basil at 49KB vs the six ~16KB documents).

I have all the data I need. The file sizes confirm Option C's structural finding (basil 49KB, peppermint 28KB vs six documents at 15-18KB). The moderator validation is clear. Here is my critique:

{"critic_id":"critic-product","critiques":[{"target_option":"Option A","weakest_assumption":"That the corpus's heterogeneity is best tamed by relational normalisation — but the dominant content is 674 structured absences plus qualitative guidance, not numeric intervals.","likely_failure_mode":"The team spends its build budget on a schema that elegantly models claims, intervals, and measurement bases for 3-5 cells while 94% of the table is NULL or placeholder rows whose semantic content is 'we have guidance text, not a number'.","hidden_cost":"Curator labour: every absence must be inserted as a typed claim with a role, scope, and provenance record. This converts a one-line '직접 근거 없음' into a multi-column row whose only queryable payload is 'absent'. That is bookkeeping for bookkeeping's sake.","best_fix":"Invert the primary entity: model the guidance action (section 9 prose + coverage status) as the first-class row, and attach a scored interval as an optional child only when one exists. The safety constraints on unit/basis conflation survive; the schema just stops pretending numbers are the main content.","verdict":"revise"},{"target_option":"Option B","weakest_assumption":"That a JSON payload 'preserves the caveat whole' — but a guidance-first product needs those caveats surfaced, filtered, and rendered, not entombed in an opaque blob.","likely_failure_mode":"The frontend must parse heterogeneous JSON shapes to display anything useful, so the schema's simplicity relocates to application-layer complexity that is harder to test. Scorability via generated columns and LIKE patterns over JSON breaks silently the first time a new status tag is added.","hidden_cost":"Markdown-as-canonical-truth means every query depends on a parse step whose correctness is validated only at query time. There is no compile-time contract between the markdown structure and the cache schema, so a document edit can silently break the dashboard.","best_fix":"Promote the three to five fields the product actually queries (coverage_status, disposition, action_text) out of the JSON blob into typed columns. Keep the blob for genuine long-tail metadata, but stop routing the hot path through it.","verdict":"revise"},{"target_option":"Option C","weakest_assumption":"That deterministic parsing plus LLM-assisted candidate generation plus mandatory human review is a feasible pipeline for a hackathon — this is a production data-engineering workflow proposed on a prototype timeline.","likely_failure_mode":"The human review bottleneck produces either a rushed rubber-stamp (defeating the safety goal) or a half-populated dataset that ships with fewer records than the simpler alternatives. The agent's own structural finding — that deterministic parsing is unsafe for 2 of 8 documents — means 25% of crops require the slow path from day one.","hidden_cost":"Hash-gating, stable record IDs, source-span locators, and JSONL intermediate format are four separate integrity mechanisms. Each must be maintained, and each can drift. The auditability promise is only as strong as the weakest link, and on a hackathon none will be tested under revision.","best_fix":"Strip the pipeline to its one genuinely valuable idea — human-approved extraction with provenance — and implement it as a curated JSON file reviewed in a PR diff, not as a multi-stage pipeline with hash gates. You get the same safety at a fraction of the build cost.","verdict":"reject"},{"target_option":"Option D","weakest_assumption":"That one row per crop x axis is sufficient — its own author concedes it cannot represent basil having both an optimum and an injury threshold on air temperature, and the validation shows welsh_onion needs a daily-mean qualifier on the same axis.","likely_failure_mode":"The concession is fatal in practice, not just in theory: the product's core value proposition is distinguishing 'you are outside the optimum' from 'you are in the injury zone', and a schema that cannot hold both makes that distinction an application-layer hack. The JSON blob becomes the real schema, un-queryable by SQL.","hidden_cost":"Hand-curation with no reproducibility means the database silently desyncs from the documents the moment a crop file is revised. With 94% empty cells the cost of a re-sync seems low, but the remaining 6% is exactly the safety-critical content.","best_fix":"Allow multiple rows per crop x axis, keyed by claim_purpose (optimum vs injury vs disease-risk). This costs ~30 minutes of schema change and converts a fatal concession into a handled case. Then accept the flat table for everything else.","verdict":"revise"},{"target_option":"Option E","weakest_assumption":"That a three-valued disposition (SCORABLE / ADVISORY_ONLY / RECORD_ONLY) is a product feature rather than an internal implementation detail — users do not care why a channel is unscorable, they care what to do about it.","likely_failure_mode":"The disposition taxonomy becomes a second maintenance surface that must track both the evidence base and the hardware configuration. With 94% of cells non-scorable, almost everything is advisory or record-only, making the three tiers a complicated way of saying 'we mostly cannot score things'.","hidden_cost":"The validation example (canopy RH scorable for disease-risk, ambient RH advisory for growth) requires the product to model use-case contexts that the current UI has no affordance for. The disposition is correct in theory and unshippable in the current product surface.","best_fix":"Collapse to two states — HAS_EVIDENCE and NO_EVIDENCE — derived directly from the coverage_status already in the data. Defer the disease-risk vs growth distinction to a later release when the UI can represent it.","verdict":"revise"},{"target_option":"Option F","weakest_assumption":"That three parallel layers (score, coverage_status, guidance action) will make users feel the dashboard is full — but 6 of 8 cards showing only prose and a coverage enum is a guidance page, not a dashboard. Calling it a dashboard sets expectations the content cannot meet.","likely_failure_mode":"The section 9 guidance is written in Korean for professional gardeners. Without translation and adaptation, the 'actionable prose' layer is unreadable to the target user. Nobody has budgeted that work, so the layer ships empty or untranslated, and the three-tier design collapses to one tier.","hidden_cost":"The pre-committed thresholds embedded in this option have now been crossed: the author said 3 or fewer scorable cells means 'abandon numeric scoring entirely'. The measured count is 3 solid. Honouring that threshold means this option's own author would reject its scored tier.","best_fix":"Accept the threshold honestly: drop the scored tier as the primary UI element and lead with guidance + telemetry graph. Keep scored bands as an accent on the 3 crops that support them, not as the organising principle of every card.","verdict":"revise"},{"target_option":"Option G","weakest_assumption":"That immutable revision tracking for ~200 facts justifies a second SQLite file, ATTACH management, and an append-only revision table — on a hackathon where the knowledge base will be written once and read until demo day.","likely_failure_mode":"Two-file architecture is build cost with zero demo payoff. Revision tracking is only meaningful if the extraction pipeline runs more than once, and Option C's human-review bottleneck makes that unlikely during the hackathon. The provenance claim is hollow if extraction is LLM-assisted and non-deterministic.","hidden_cost":"Splitting telemetry and knowledge means every query that joins a reading to its threshold crosses a database boundary. SQLite's ATTACH works but adds a fragile operational dependency and makes deployment packaging harder.","best_fix":"Ship one file. Add a single 'extracted_at' timestamp column to the fact table for lightweight provenance. Defer the split to a post-hackathon milestone when write contention actually materialises.","verdict":"reject"},{"target_option":"Option H","weakest_assumption":"That corpus-boundedness is an acceptable scope — well-known horticultural references (e.g. RH 60-80% for most crops) were deliberately excluded, and whether that exclusion was correct is unassessed. The product may be dying of a self-imposed evidence wound.","likely_failure_mode":"Stakeholders hear 'this is not an environment-score product' and respond by overriding with invented thresholds, which is exactly the failure the corpus was built to prevent. The reframing is scientifically honest but politically undefended.","hidden_cost":"Even for the 3 scorable crops, air temperature without duration data produces alerts that flap on every transient excursion. A condition monitor that cries wolf on momentary spikes will be ignored or disabled, making the honest product useless in practice.","best_fix":"Pair the reframing with a concrete fallback product spec: 'condition monitor with telemetry graphs, qualitative guidance cards, and scored range-membership for 3 crops'. Name the product what it is in the stakeholder pitch so the reframing has a positive identity, not just a negation. Add a hysteresis buffer (e.g. 5-minute rolling average) to the range check to suppress flapping without requiring duration data.","verdict":"proceed"}],"overall_ranking":["Option H","Option D","Option F","Option B","Option E","Option A","Option C","Option G"],"decision_crux":"The environment-score dashboard is dead as specified: 3 of 8 crops have zero scorable axes and 94% of the 64-cell matrix is empty, crossing both pre-committed abandonment thresholds. The honest product is a condition monitor with telemetry, qualitative guidance, and scored range-membership for 3 crops only. That verdict simplifies the schema question rather than complicating it — a guidance-first product needs differently-shaped schema (guidance actions and coverage statuses as first-class entities, scored intervals as optional children) but far less schema depth than a scoring engine. Option D plus Option H's reframing plus Option F's guidance layer (minus its scored-tier pretence) is the minimum viable combination: a flat table extended to multiple rows per axis, guidance text promoted to a queryable column, and an honest product name that does not promise scores it cannot deliver."}
